        "uuid": "6272401f_77cb4a26",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 22,
      "author": {
        "id": 5201498
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-17T11:11:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree that it\u0027s a big change. But I don\u0027t think the removal of key generate/import/prepare related interfaces affects the inline encryption implementation in virtio block as we can use the hardware-wrapped key generated via keymaster/keymint. Thus, this change can work on both android16-6.12 and the newer kernel version.",
      "parentUuid": "754fdc63_8a557e2c",
      "revId": "61d8dfad035d9450dc82a94c6d8e47cf8e494a71",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "040fb5ea_fa2c3f4a",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 22,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137063
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-19T13:29:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The Keymint based implementation of generate/import/prepare is not compatible with the upstream kernel and is deprecated in favor of the Linux kernel ioctls.  So we should not be building new systems on top of it.",
      "parentUuid": "6272401f_77cb4a26",
      "revId": "61d8dfad035d9450dc82a94c6d8e47cf8e494a71",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d212083d_b5bf57cc",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 22,
      "author": {
        "id": 5201498
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-20T09:18:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks. I think there may be two separate aspects here: key generation/preparation and the virtio-blk inline encryption interface.\n\nThe KeyMint-based implementation is involved in wrapped-key generation/import and preparation. However, the proposed virtio-blk interface is not limited to those operations. It also defines how a prepared key is programmed into or evicted from a keyslot, as well as how encrypted I/O is submitted. These operations are independent of whether the key was generated/prepared through KeyMint or through the Linux kernel ioctl interface.\n\nTaking the raw-key case as a simple example, although raw keys are generally not used in our deployment, the same virtio-blk keyslot-management commands and encrypted I/O path would still apply without involving KeyMint-based key generation/preparation.\n\nCould you clarify whether your concern is specifically about the key provisioning mechanism, or whether you see a dependency on KeyMint in the proposed virtio-blk interface itself?",
      "parentUuid": "040fb5ea_fa2c3f4a",
      "revId": "61d8dfad035d9450dc82a94c6d8e47cf8e494a71",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
